@page "/rules"
@using Checkers.Shared.Enums
@using Checkers.Shared.GameModels

<PageTitle>Rules &amp; Variants</PageTitle>

<div class="rules-page">
    <h1>Rules &amp; Variants</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Checkers is played around the world under different rules. When you create a match you pick a
        <strong>game type</strong> (a preset of rules for one national variant) and can change any single rule under
        <em>Customize rules</em>. This page explains each variant and what every rule does.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="basics">
        <h2 id="basics">The basics (every variant)</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The board is checkered and only the dark squares are used. Black starts at the top, White at the bottom.</li>
            <li><strong>Men</strong> move one square diagonally forwards, onto an empty square.</li>
            <li>You <strong>capture</strong> by jumping diagonally over an opponent's piece onto the empty square beyond it. The jumped piece is removed.</li>
            <li>If the piece that just captured can capture again, it keeps going in the same turn: a <strong>multi-jump</strong>. Once a multi-jump has started it must be finished.</li>
            <li>A man that reaches the far row is <strong>crowned</strong> and becomes a <strong>king</strong>. Kings move and capture backwards as well as forwards.</li>
            <li>You <strong>win</strong> when your opponent has no legal move, either because they have no pieces left or because every piece is blocked.</li>
            <li>You can also win when your opponent <strong>resigns</strong> or runs out of time, and the game is a <strong>draw</strong> if both players agree to one.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="compare">
        <h2 id="compare">The variants at a glance</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Variant comparison, scrolls sideways">
            <table class="compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Variant</th>
                        <th scope="col">Board</th>
                        <th scope="col">Men each</th>
                        <th scope="col">First</th>
                        <th scope="col">Captures</th>
                        <th scope="col">Which capture</th>
                        <th scope="col">Men capture backwards</th>
                        <th scope="col">Men take kings</th>
                        <th scope="col">Kings</th>
                        <th scope="col">Crowned mid-capture</th>
                        <th scope="col">Captured pieces removed</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var preset in GameVariants.All)
                    {
                        var r = preset.Rules;
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">@preset.Name</th>
                            <td>@r.BoardSize × @r.BoardSize</td>
                            <td>@r.PiecesPerSide</td>
                            <td>@r.FirstPlayer</td>
                            <td>@(r.ForcedCaptures ? "Forced" : "Optional")</td>
                            <td>@CaptureChoiceShort(r.CaptureChoice)</td>
                            <td>@YesNo(r.MenCaptureBackward)</td>
                            <td>@YesNo(r.MenCanCaptureKings)</td>
                            <td>@KingsShort(r)</td>
                            <td>@PromotionShort(r.PromotionDuringCapture)</td>
                            <td>@(r.RemoveCapturedAtEndOfMove ? "After the move" : "At once")</td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="rules">
        <h2 id="rules">What each rule does</h2>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Board size and rows of men</h3>
            <p>
                Most variants use an 8 × 8 board with 3 rows of men each (12 men a side). International draughts uses
                10 × 10 with 4 rows (20 men), Canadian uses 12 × 12 with 5 rows (30 men), and Thai uses the 8 × 8 board
                with only 2 rows (8 men). The two middle rows always start empty.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Who moves first</h3>
            <p>
                American checkers starts with Black; most European variants start with White. Black always sits at the top
                of the board and moves down, White at the bottom moving up, whoever goes first.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Forced captures</h3>
            <dl>
                <dt>On (every preset except Casual)</dt>
                <dd>If you can capture, you must. A move that doesn't capture is refused while a capture is available.</dd>
                <dt>Off (Casual)</dt>
                <dd>Capturing is optional, so you may make an ordinary move instead. Once you start a multi-jump you must still finish it.</dd>
            </dl>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Which capture you must take</h3>
            <p>When more than one capture is possible, this decides which ones you're allowed to choose.</p>
            <dl>
                <dt>Any capture</dt>
                <dd>Choose any capture you like, as long as you carry it through to the end. (American, Russian, Pool, Thai)</dd>
                <dt>Most pieces</dt>
                <dd>You must take the capture that removes the most pieces. Men and kings count the same. (International, Brazilian, Canadian)</dd>
                <dt>Most pieces, then most kings</dt>
                <dd>The most pieces first; if two captures take the same number, the one that takes more kings. (Spanish)</dd>
                <dt>Italian priority</dt>
                <dd>
                    In order: the most pieces; then capturing with a king rather than a man; then the most kings taken;
                    then the capture that takes a king soonest. (Italian)
                </dd>
                <dt>Kings capture first</dt>
                <dd>If any of your kings can capture, you must capture with a king; otherwise any capture. (Czech)</dd>
            </dl>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Men capturing backwards</h3>
            <p>
                Men always <em>move</em> forwards only. In Russian, Pool, Brazilian, International and Canadian draughts they
                may also <em>capture</em> backwards, which makes multi-jumps that zig-zag back down the board possible.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Men capturing kings</h3>
            <p>
                Normally a man can capture any piece. In Italian draughts a man can't jump a king: only a king can capture a king.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Flying kings</h3>
            <dl>
                <dt>Off (American, Italian)</dt>
                <dd>Kings move one square diagonally, in any direction, and capture by jumping an adjacent piece.</dd>
                <dt>On (most other variants)</dt>
                <dd>
                    Kings move any number of empty squares along a diagonal, like a bishop in chess. They can capture a piece
                    any distance away along the diagonal and land on any empty square beyond it.
                </dd>
                <dt>Land right behind the capture (Thai)</dt>
                <dd>A flying king still captures from a distance, but must land on the square directly behind the captured piece.</dd>
            </dl>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Reaching the far row in the middle of a capture</h3>
            <dl>
                <dt>Crowned, and the move ends (American, Spanish, Italian, Czech, Thai)</dt>
                <dd>The man becomes a king, and the move stops there even if the new king could capture again.</dd>
                <dt>Crowned, and keeps capturing as a king (Russian)</dt>
                <dd>The man becomes a king on the spot and carries on its multi-jump with the king's powers.</dd>
                <dt>Keeps capturing as a man (International, Brazilian, Canadian, Pool)</dt>
                <dd>
                    The man carries on capturing as a man. It's only crowned if its move ends on the far row; if it jumps
                    back out again, it stays a man.
                </dd>
            </dl>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>When captured pieces come off the board</h3>
            <dl>
                <dt>At once (American, Italian, Thai)</dt>
                <dd>Each piece is removed as soon as it's jumped.</dd>
                <dt>After the move (most flying-king variants)</dt>
                <dd>
                    Captured pieces stay on the board until the whole multi-jump is finished. They still block the way, and
                    the same piece can't be jumped twice in one move. This matters most for flying kings, which could
                    otherwise loop back across a square they've already cleared.
                </dd>
            </dl>
        </article>

        <article class="rule">
            <h3>Turn time limit</h3>
            <p>
                Separate from the variant, a match can have a turn time limit anywhere from 30 seconds to 1 week. The clock
                starts when your opponent joins and restarts each time the turn passes. It keeps running through a
                multi-jump and doesn't pause if you disconnect. A player who doesn't finish their turn in time
                automatically resigns.
            </p>
        </article>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="variants">
        <h2 id="variants">The variants</h2>
        <div class="variant-grid">
            @foreach (var preset in GameVariants.All)
            {
                <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Outlined" class="variant-card">
                    <h3>@preset.Name</h3>
                    <p>@preset.Description</p>
                    <ul class="variant-rules">
                        @foreach (var detail in Details(preset.Rules))
                        {
                            <li>@detail</li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                    <RadzenButton Text="Play this variant" Icon="sports_esports" Size="ButtonSize.Small" ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                                  Click="@(() => Play(preset.Variant))" />
                </RadzenCard>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="not-covered">
        <h2 id="not-covered">Not covered (yet)</h2>
        <p>
            Some variants have extra draw rules: a stronger side must win within a set number of moves, or the game is drawn
            when the same position repeats three times. These aren't applied here; a draw is only by agreement. Spanish and
            Italian draughts are traditionally played with the board turned the other way, and Italian men may capture at
            most three pieces in a row; neither is applied. Turkish draughts, where pieces move in straight lines, isn't offered.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
